 "caution": "18.4 million words, versus hundreds of millions for the main languages. Frequency values are unreliable, so there are no frequency bands, only the all-words generators. The reference lexicon has 44,447 words, so generators, neighbourhood measures and cluster filters are rougher than for the main languages. Japanese pseudowords are katakana strings and the unit is the mora: a kana, or a kana with a small kana (キャ, ファ, ティ); ッ, ン and ー are morae of their own. Every word of the subtitle list (kanji, hiragana, katakana) was converted to katakana with pykakasi and split into 146 morae, and the pipeline's letters are these morae: length, OLD20, Coltheart's N (one mora exchanged, added or removed) and the n-gram models count morae. CV is the structure of each mora in turn: a vowel kana or ー = V, ン and ッ = C, every other mora = CV (so カン = CVC, キャット = CVCCV). Readings come from the KAKASI dictionary and can be wrong for rare kanji compounds; long vowels keep their kana spelling (オウ in native words, ー in loanwords).",
